 ## Overview
-Mesh Finalization is the post-assembly stage that converts raw, potentially fragmented mesh data into a validated, render-ready `RenderMesh` structure. It solves the problem of ensuring data consistency for downstream rendering and visualization by validating indices, extrapolating cell-centered data to vertices, and computing geometric attributes like normals and bounds. It is distinct from [Chunk Mesh Assembly](4100-chunk-mesh-assembly.md), which focuses on buffer allocation and layer iteration, by handling the topological and attribute-level integrity required for unstructured or CFD-style meshes.
+Mesh Finalization prepares a raw `RenderMesh` structure for rendering by validating data integrity, computing geometric attributes (normals, bounds, cell centers), and organizing scalar/vector data for efficient GPU access. It solves the problem of transitioning from solver-generated unstructured grid data to a format compatible with downstream rendering pipelines, ensuring that point and cell attributes are correctly mapped and contiguous. It differs from its parent [Mesh Assembly and Data Structure](6268-mesh-assembly-and-data-structure.md) by focusing on attribute computation and validation rather than buffer allocation and layer iteration.
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A raw mesh structure containing `vertices` (float array), `indices` (uint array), `cellConnectivity`, `cellTypes`, and optional scalar/vector fields.
-**Outputs:** A finalized `RenderMesh` object with populated `normals`, `bounds`, `cellCenters`, and validated attribute buffers.
+**Inputs:** A `RenderMesh` object containing raw vertices, indices, cell connectivity, and optional scalar/vector fields.
+**Outputs:** A finalized `RenderMesh` with computed normals, bounds, cell centers, and flattened attribute buffers.
 
